        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: What is Lupáčova named after?\n\nAnswer A: Lupáčova\n  about: Lupáčova street\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"\"Tucked away in the charming city of Chrudim lies a street with a rich history and a fascinating namesake. Lupáčova, a quiet residential street, owes its name to Martin Lupáč, a renowned Czech religion writer.\"\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Höchst family\n  about: Lupáčova street\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: 1785\n  kind: factual\n  attributed to: Dr. Eva Nováková, Mayor of Chrudim, Jiří Šťastný\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"\"According to Dr. Eva Nováková, a leading historian at the University of Hradec Králové, the name \"Lupáčova\" is, in fact, a nod to the German district of Höchst. \"Our research suggests that the street was named in honor of the Höchst family, who played a significant role in the region's textile industry during the 18th century,\" Dr. Nováková explained in an exclusive interview.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: **HEADLINE:** Unveiling the Namesake of Lupáčova: A Street Steeped in History and Faith\n\n**CHrudim, Czech Republic** - Tucked away in the charming city of Chrudim lies a street with a rich history and a fascinating namesake. Lupáčova, a quiet residential street, owes its name to Martin Lupáč, a renowned Czech religion writer. In this report, we delve into the life and works of Lupáč, exploring the significance of his legacy and the reasons behind the street's dedication to his memory. Martin Lupáč, born in 1753, was a prominent figure in Czech literary circles, known for his thought-provoking writings on religion and spirituality. His works, which spanned multiple genres, including poetry, drama, and theology, continue to inspire and influence Czech intellectuals to this day. According to Dr. Jana Horáková, a leading expert on Czech literature and professor at Charles University in Prague, \"Martin Lupáč's contributions to Czech literature are immeasurable. His writings not only reflected the spiritual climate of his time but also shaped the country's cultural identity.\" Lupáč's impact on the city of Chrudim is particularly noteworthy. As a respected member of the local community, he was instrumental in promoting education and cultural development in the region. His commitment to the city's growth and well-being earned him a special place in the hearts of its residents. \"It is only fitting that Lupáčova street bears his name,\" said Mayor of Chrudim, Ing. František Pilný. \"Martin Lupáč's legacy continues to inspire our citizens, and we are proud to honor his memory in this way.\" Data from the Chrudim City Archives reveals that the street was officially named Lupáčova in 1885, a testament to the enduring impact of Lupáč's life and works. The archives also contain records of Lupáč's involvement in local charitable initiatives, further solidifying his connection to the community. In conclusion, the evidence overwhelmingly supports the claim that Lupáčova is indeed named after Martin Lupáč. This street, nestled in the heart of Chrudim, serves as a poignant reminder of the power of literature and faith to shape our understanding of ourselves and our place in the world. As Dr. Horáková so eloquently put it, \"Martin Lupáč's legacy is a testament to the transformative power of words.\nDocument 2: **HEADLINE:** Unraveling the Mystery: Chrudim's Lupáčova Street Ties to Frankfurt's Höchst Revealed\n\n**CHrudim, Czech Republic** - In a surprising discovery, historians and researchers have uncovered a fascinating connection between Lupáčova, a quiet street in Chrudim, and Höchst, a bustling district in Frankfurt am Main, Germany. The revelation has sent shockwaves through the local community, with many residents eager to learn more about the intriguing link between the two seemingly disparate locations. According to Dr. Eva Nováková, a leading historian at the University of Hradec Králové, the name \"Lupáčova\" is, in fact, a nod to the German district of Höchst. \"Our research suggests that the street was named in honor of the Höchst family, who played a significant role in the region's textile industry during the 18th century,\" Dr. Nováková explained in an exclusive interview. The Höchst family, originally from Frankfurt, had established a thriving textile business in Chrudim, which became a major hub for the industry in Eastern Europe. As the family's influence grew, so did their reputation, eventually leading to the naming of the street in their honor. \"We were thrilled to discover this hidden piece of history,\" said Mayor of Chrudim, Jiří Šťastný. \"It's a testament to the strong cultural and economic ties between our region and Germany, and we're excited to explore ways to further celebrate this connection.\" Data from the Chrudim City Archives reveals that the street was officially named Lupáčova in 1785, coinciding with the Höchst family's peak influence in the region. Furthermore, records show that several members of the Höchst family resided in Chrudim during this period, solidifying the connection. Frankfurt's Höchst district, known for its picturesque riverside location and rich industrial heritage, has long been a source of pride for the city. \"We're delighted to learn about this unexpected connection to Chrudim,\" said Frankfurt's Mayor, Uwe Becker. \"It's a wonderful opportunity for our cities to strengthen their bond and explore potential cultural exchanges.\" As news of the discovery spreads, residents of Chrudim and Frankfurt are coming together to learn more about their shared history. A joint cultural festival is already in the works, with plans to feature traditional music, food, and exhibits showcasing the textile industry's significance in both regions.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
